DwarfSpec Event Awaiting Implementation
========================================

Purpose:
    ☐ Add `ds.awaitEvent(event, options)` for awaiting the next supported Dwarf Fortress event without polling a state predicate.
    ☐ Support the seven core Lua-visible DFHack state-change events needed by deterministic automation.
    ☐ Replace the command-specific map-loaded listener with the shared event-awaiting command.

Completion contract:
    ☐ Complete every applicable implementation, focused-test, full-test, declaration, documentation, package, cleanup, and live-evidence item below.
    ☐ Treat this checklist as the source of truth for implementation status.
    ☐ Preserve unrelated worktree and index changes; do not stash or commit user changes.
    ☐ Keep focused unit, full unit, Lua syntax, package, cleanup, and live DFHack evidence distinct in the final handoff.
    ☐ Keep EventManager events and specialized `eventful` hooks outside this implementation unless this checklist is explicitly revised.

Command contract:
    ☐ Export `ds.awaitEvent(event, options)`.
    ☐ Define a public `ds.EEvent` enum with `WORLD_LOADED`, `WORLD_UNLOADED`, `MAP_LOADED`, `MAP_UNLOADED`, `VIEWSCREEN_CHANGED`, `PAUSED`, and `UNPAUSED`.
    ☐ Wait for the next matching event; do not infer success from the associated state already being true.
    ☐ Accept an optional `trigger` callback that runs only after the native listener is armed.
    ☐ Capture a matching event raised synchronously by `trigger`.
    ☐ Accept an optional nonempty diagnostic `description`.
    ☐ Accept an optional positive `timeout_ms`, with `false` disabling the command-local timeout.
    ☐ Do not impose a command-local timeout by default.
    ☐ Return one immutable event-occurrence record containing the public event, source, and normalized payload.
    ☐ Never expose a transient native pointer through the returned occurrence.
    ☐ Resume the waiting test at a safe scheduler boundary instead of resuming it inside the DFHack callback.
    ☐ Remove the native listener after success, trigger failure, timeout, cancellation, example cleanup, or run cleanup.
    ☐ Ignore unrelated state-change events without consuming the wait.
    ☐ Reject unsupported events and malformed options with bounded diagnostics.

Normalized payload contract:
    ☐ Return the loaded save-directory name for `WORLD_LOADED` and `MAP_LOADED` when it is available.
    ☐ Capture the previous save-directory name for `WORLD_UNLOADED` and `MAP_UNLOADED` before it becomes unavailable.
    ☐ Return the current focus and native screen type for `VIEWSCREEN_CHANGED` without returning the screen object.
    ☐ Return `paused=true` for `PAUSED`.
    ☐ Return `paused=false` for `UNPAUSED`.
    ☐ Define and test the result when an otherwise valid payload field is unavailable.

Non-goals:
    ☐ Do not replace `ds.await()` for polling arbitrary read-only conditions.
    ☐ Do not replace `ds.wait_frames()` or `ds.wait_ticks()`.
    ☐ Do not replay past events or treat current state as an event occurrence.
    ☐ Do not expose `SC_CORE_INITIALIZED`, since a DwarfSpec command cannot normally await it before it occurs.
    ☐ Do not expose the C++-only `SC_BEGIN_UNLOAD` or the `SC_UNKNOWN` sentinel.
    ☐ Do not add arbitrary plugin-event discovery or string-based callback registration.
    ☐ Do not enable, disable, or change shared `eventful` EventManager frequencies.
    ☐ Do not expose synchronous workshop, reaction, wound, or projectile interposition hooks.

Phase 0 - Freeze the runtime contract and baseline:
    ☒ Record the active checkout, branch, worktree state, index state, installed rock version, and current package archive contents.
    ☒ Reconfirm the state-change constants exported by the active DFHack Lua runtime.
    ☒ Reconfirm that `dfhack.onStateChange` supports keyed listeners that can be removed independently.
    ☒ Record the current `ds.await()`, `ds.wait_frames()`, and `ds.wait_ticks()` declarations, implementations, and scheduler wait kinds.
    ☒ Record the current command-specific `await_native_map_loaded()` implementation and every caller.
    ☒ Identify the scheduler cancellation and run-cleanup paths that must release an event listener.
    ☒ Select the production and test filenames before implementation.
    ☒ Run and record the focused scheduler and save-game mount unit baselines.
    ☒ Run and record the full standalone unit baseline.
    ☒ Run and record the pre-change Lua syntax and formatting baseline.
    ☒ Stop and revise this checklist if the active runtime does not expose the seven contracted events.

Phase 1 - Add the public event types:
    ☐ Define the public `dwarfspec.EEvent` enum in `src/ds.d.lua`.
    ☐ Define `dwarfspec.EventWaitOptions`.
        ☐ Type the optional `trigger` callback.
        ☐ Type the optional `description`.
        ☐ Type `timeout_ms` as a positive integer or `false`.
    ☐ Define the immutable `dwarfspec.EventOccurrence` record.
        ☐ Type the public `event`.
        ☐ Type the fixed `state_change` source.
        ☐ Type the normalized payload.
    ☐ Declare `ds.awaitEvent(event, options)`.
    ☐ Document next-occurrence semantics at the declaration.
    ☐ Document listener-before-trigger ordering at the declaration.
    ☐ Document the absence of a default command-local timeout.
    ☐ Add language-appropriate documentation comments for every new production method and type.
    ☐ Add `EEvent` and `awaitEvent` to the reserved DwarfSpec command names.
    ☐ Add declaration fixtures or declaration checks for valid and invalid calls if the current declaration test harness supports them.

Phase 2 - Implement scheduler event suspension:
    ☐ Add a dedicated event wait kind to the coroutine scheduler.
    ☐ Give each event wait a unique wait identity that cannot collide with another run or example.
    ☐ Add a signal path that accepts an immutable occurrence for the currently outstanding event wait.
    ☐ Queue a signaled occurrence for the next safe scheduler pump.
    ☐ Do not call `coroutine.resume()` from inside a DFHack event callback.
    ☐ Preserve an occurrence signaled synchronously before the waiting coroutine finishes suspending.
    ☐ Ignore duplicate signals after the first accepted occurrence.
    ☐ Ignore signals for stale or canceled wait identities.
    ☐ Apply an explicit positive timeout only when supplied.
    ☐ Support `timeout_ms=false`.
    ☐ Leave the command-local timeout disabled when the option is omitted.
    ☐ Ensure scheduler cancellation invokes the wait's listener-cleanup callback exactly once.
    ☐ Include the operation description, awaited event, elapsed time, and current runtime diagnostics in timeout errors.
    ☐ Add focused scheduler tests for synchronous signaling, deferred resumption, unrelated signals, duplicate signals, timeout, cancellation, stale signals, and exactly-once cleanup.

Phase 3 - Implement the core state-change command:
    ☐ Add `src/dwarfspec/commands/await_event.lua`.
    ☐ Keep DFHack listener registration and payload normalization in the command module.
    ☐ Map each public enum value to its exact DFHack state-change constant.
    ☐ Validate the event and option record before installing a listener.
    ☐ Install one uniquely keyed `dfhack.onStateChange` listener for the wait.
    ☐ Capture the currently loaded save-directory name before unload events can erase it.
    ☐ Snapshot payload data while it is valid.
    ☐ Arm the native listener before invoking `trigger`.
    ☐ Forward a matching normalized occurrence to the scheduler event signal path.
    ☐ Remove the listener after the first accepted occurrence.
    ☐ Remove the listener when `trigger` raises an error.
    ☐ Preserve the original trigger error after listener cleanup.
    ☐ Expose a concise documented construction function for run-scoped dependency binding.
    ☐ Keep dependency injection available for focused tests without redefining unrelated `ds` commands.
    ☐ Add focused command-module tests for all seven mappings and payloads.
    ☐ Add focused command-module tests for validation, listener ordering, synchronous events, unrelated events, trigger failure, unavailable payload fields, and listener cleanup.

Phase 4 - Export the command and migrate map loading:
    ☐ Construct the event-awaiting command with the current run's scheduler and native DFHack dependencies.
    ☐ Export `ds.EEvent` without allowing consumers to mutate the authoritative internal mapping.
    ☐ Export `ds.awaitEvent(event, options)` from the run-scoped `ds` namespace.
    ☐ Keep the public implementation limited to validation, dependency delegation, and its documented return value.
    ☐ Replace `await_native_map_loaded()` with `ds.awaitEvent(ds.EEvent.MAP_LOADED, {trigger=...})` or the equivalent shared internal command call.
    ☐ Remove the command-specific map-loaded listener counter, boolean observation, and query polling.
    ☐ Preserve `ds.mountSaveGame()` listener-before-save-selection ordering.
    ☐ Preserve `ds.mountSaveGame()` behavior, return value, no-op path, and lack of example-owned unload cleanup.
    ☐ Confirm that no other `ds` command changes behavior.
    ☐ Add export, delegation, and save-game-loader migration coverage.

Phase 5 - Verify focused and full unit behavior:
    ☐ Prove that `awaitEvent()` waits for a new occurrence when the corresponding state is already true.
    ☐ Prove that the listener is armed before `trigger`.
    ☐ Prove that a synchronous trigger event is not lost.
    ☐ Prove that unrelated events do not resume the wait.
    ☐ Prove that only the first matching event is returned.
    ☐ Prove that every success and failure path removes the listener exactly once.
    ☐ Prove that canceled or stale listeners cannot resume a later example.
    ☐ Prove that returned payloads contain snapshots rather than native pointers.
    ☐ Prove all seven public event-to-state-change mappings.
    ☐ Prove that `ds.mountSaveGame()` uses the shared map-loaded event wait.
    ☐ Run and record focused scheduler tests.
    ☐ Run and record focused command-module tests.
    ☐ Run and record focused save-game mount tests.
    ☐ Run and record the full standalone unit suite separately.
    ☐ Run and record Lua syntax and formatting checks separately.
    ☐ Run and record `git diff --check`.

Phase 6 - Verify live DFHack behavior:
    ☐ Add focused live coverage for `PAUSED` and `UNPAUSED` using DFHack's built-in simulated input.
    ☐ Restore the original pause state even when a live assertion fails.
    ☐ Add focused live coverage for `VIEWSCREEN_CHANGED` using a controlled native-screen transition.
    ☐ Restore or dismiss every screen opened by the live test.
    ☐ Reuse the dynamic smallest-save selection based on `world.sav` size for load and unload coverage.
    ☐ Do not depend on a hardcoded save-directory name.
    ☐ Add focused live coverage for `MAP_LOADED` through `ds.mountSaveGame()`.
    ☐ Add focused live coverage for the applicable world/map unload and load notifications.
    ☐ Use native events or explicit state predicates for setup and cleanup; do not add arbitrary sleep periods.
    ☐ Confirm that no physical mouse manipulation is used.
    ☐ Confirm every selected live run reports `cleanup_confirmed=true`.
    ☐ Confirm the executor is idle, the queue is empty, quarantine is absent, mounts are clear, pointer tracking is restored, no buttons remain pressed, and DFHack remains responsive.
    ☐ Record selected live-event results separately from the complete live suite.

Phase 7 - Document, package, and close acceptance:
    ☐ Add `ds.awaitEvent()` and `ds.EEvent` to the public command documentation.
    ☐ Document all seven supported events and their normalized payloads.
    ☐ Document next-occurrence semantics and contrast them with `ds.await()` current-state polling.
    ☐ Document listener-before-trigger ordering with a save-loading example.
    ☐ Document optional timeout behavior and the absence of a default command-local timeout.
    ☐ Document that EventManager and specialized `eventful` callbacks are not supported by this release.
    ☐ Update the LuaRocks package manifest for every new production module and declaration artifact.
    ☐ Build the release rock through the repository publish tooling.
    ☐ Inspect the archive contents directly.
    ☐ Require the event command module and updated declaration artifact in the archive.
    ☐ Reject tests, fixture saves, generated logs, and temporary artifacts from the archive.
    ☐ Load the built artifact in the controlled DFHack environment.
    ☐ Re-run the focused live event coverage against the packaged artifact.
    ☐ Re-run the save-game mount coverage against the packaged artifact.
    ☐ Run and record the complete live suite separately.
    ☐ Record the final diff, worktree, index, archive, and DFHack runtime state.

Deferred event families:
    EventManager candidates:
        - `JOB_INITIATED`, `JOB_STARTED`, `JOB_COMPLETED`
        - `UNIT_NEW_ACTIVE`, `UNIT_DEATH`
        - `ITEM_CREATED`, `BUILDING`, `CONSTRUCTION`
        - `SYNDROME`, `INVASION`, `INVENTORY_CHANGE`, `REPORT`
        - `UNIT_ATTACK`, `UNLOAD`, `INTERACTION`
    Required design work before EventManager support:
        - Define immutable payloads for callbacks that currently expose native pointers.
        - Define safe ownership for globally shared `eventful` scan frequencies.
        - Add frequency introspection and restoration or use a native adapter with per-consumer ownership.
        - Avoid duplicating `ds.wait_ticks()` for the EventManager `TICK` type.
    Specialized hooks intentionally excluded from the generic event API:
        - `onWorkshopFillSidebarMenu`, `postWorkshopFillSidebarMenu`
        - `onReactionCompleting`, `onReactionComplete`
        - `onItemContaminateWound`
        - `onProjItemCheckImpact`, `onProjItemCheckMovement`
        - `onProjUnitCheckImpact`, `onProjUnitCheckMovement`

Completion evidence:
    ☒ Contract and baseline:
        - Evidence: Recorded 2026-07-30 PDT against checkout `D:\CODE\DFHack\DwarfSpec`, commit `55383880bfdb09a700f177c50bc02bf2eba60197`, branch `world-mount-system` tracking `origin/world-mount-system` at `ahead 18, behind 0`. The worktree and index were clean before this checklist update.
        - Evidence: The active LuaRocks installation is `dwarfspec 0.2.0-1`; `dwarfspec version` reports `DwarfSpec 0.2.0`. Live DFHack resolves `dwarfspec.ds` from `C:/Users/Sisco/AppData/Roaming/luarocks/share/lua/5.4/dwarfspec/ds.lua`.
        - Evidence: The current archive is `dist/dwarfspec-0.2.0-1.all.rock`, 214152 bytes, SHA-256 `7AE48475E2533849ADF2825D0CA4BEFED3F4F88B4A31D9CB55F437C9BFC3996A`. Direct `tar -tf` inspection found 97 entries: 90 `lua/`, 4 `doc/`, 1 `bin/`, and 2 root entries, with no test entry. The archive contains the declaration, scheduler, save-game load adapter and command, `ds` implementation, executable, documents, rockspec, and rock manifest.
        - Evidence: Live DFHack `53.15-r2` exported `SC_WORLD_LOADED=0`, `SC_WORLD_UNLOADED=1`, `SC_MAP_LOADED=2`, `SC_MAP_UNLOADED=3`, `SC_VIEWSCREEN_CHANGED=4`, `SC_PAUSED=7`, and `SC_UNPAUSED=8`. A read-only contract probe installed two unique `dfhack.onStateChange` keys, removed the first without disturbing the second, then removed the second; final checks reported both keys absent.
        - Evidence: Public declarations are `src/ds.d.lua`: `DS.wait_frames` at line 282, `DS.wait_ticks` at line 288, and `DS.await` at line 296. Run-scoped implementations are `src/dwarfspec/ds.lua`: `ds.wait_frames` at line 872 delegates to `wait_frames`, `ds.wait_ticks` at line 881 delegates to `wait_ticks`, and `ds.await` at line 891 delegates to `wait_until`.
        - Evidence: `src/dwarfspec/automation/coroutine_scheduler.lua` currently owns `frames`, `ticks`, and `query` waits. `wait_frames`, `wait_ticks`, and `wait_until` all suspend the sole owner; frame and query waits advance from raw-frame callbacks, while tick waits use the simulation-tick timeout callback.
        - Evidence: `src/dwarfspec/commands/save_game_load.lua:174` defines the only `await_native_map_loaded()`. It allocates a unique `dwarfspec.mountSaveGame.mapLoaded.N` key, records `SC_MAP_LOADED`, runs the selection action after listener registration, polls the recorded boolean through `wait_until`, and clears the key after success or failure. It is bound as the workflow's `await_map_loaded` dependency at line 237; `src/dwarfspec/automation/save_game_load.lua:91` calls that dependency, and its sole workflow call is the save selection at line 182. No other caller was found.
        - Evidence: Event-listener cancellation must integrate with `coroutine_scheduler.cancel()` in `src/dwarfspec/automation/coroutine_scheduler.lua:405`. Run-level release must also survive `clean_run()` in `src/dwarfspec/automation/host.lua`, which cancels the scheduler before draining the cleanup registry and is reached by both normal `finalize_run()` and abort `terminate_aborted()` paths. Example cleanup must therefore own an idempotent listener cleanup entry in the existing cleanup registry as well as the scheduler wait cleanup callback.
        - Evidence: Selected production files are new `src/dwarfspec/state_change_events.lua` and `src/dwarfspec/commands/await_event.lua`, plus existing `src/ds.d.lua`, `src/dwarfspec/automation/coroutine_scheduler.lua`, `src/dwarfspec/ds.lua`, `src/dwarfspec/commands/save_game_load.lua`, `dwarfspec-0.2.0-1.rockspec`, and `README.md`.
        - Evidence: Selected tests are new `tests/unit/await_event_command_spec.lua`, existing `tests/unit/automation_scheduler_spec.lua`, `tests/unit/immutable_enums_spec.lua`, `tests/unit/automation_entrypoint_contract_spec.lua`, and `tests/unit/save_game_load_command_spec.lua`, plus new live files `tests/automation/await_event_live_spec.lua` and `tests/destructive/await_event_world_live_spec.lua`.
        - Evidence: Focused scheduler baseline: `tools/Run-UnitTests.ps1 --filter 'automation scheduler'` completed with 12 successes, 0 failures, 0 errors, and 0 pending.
        - Evidence: Focused save-game baseline: `tools/Run-UnitTests.ps1 --filter 'save%-game'` completed with 28 successes, 0 failures, 0 errors, and 0 pending.
